Ways the change could fail a criterion (what each rating is measured against):

  smallest-edit-for-defect
    [other] smallest-edit-for-defect/descriptor-can-return-non-smallest-edit
      The descriptor stage can return an edit that changes the named defect but is not the smallest one, because the prompt and validation only require a contiguous replacement inside a named region and do not enforce minimality.
      src/acceptance/mutation/descriptor.py#0
      src/acceptance/mutation/validity.py#0
      src/acceptance/mutation/runner.py#0
    [other] smallest-edit-for-defect/verification-gate-can-block-settled-edits
      Observed kills and survivals only reach the settled tier when verification is enabled and succeeds, so the execution path can keep a valid injected edit from counting as the smallest edit that makes the defect true.
      src/acceptance/mutation/attempt.py#0
      src/acceptance/mutation/verification.py#0
      src/acceptance/mutation/settings.py#0
      src/acceptance/pipeline.py#5
      src/acceptance/report.py#1
    [other] smallest-edit-for-defect/already-present-path-repairs-instead-of-injecting
      When the descriptor says the code already has the defective behaviour, the runner switches to a repair edit instead of building an edit that makes the defect true, so the criterion can be missed for those defects.
      src/acceptance/mutation/descriptor.py#0
      src/acceptance/mutation/attempt.py#0
      src/acceptance/mutation/runner.py#0
      src/acceptance/report.py#1
    [missing_case] smallest-edit-for-defect/declines-and-not-mutable-paths-bypass-edit-building
      Defects that are declined as not a code property, not one contiguous edit, cannot tell, or not mutable never produce an injected edit, so the review can fail to build the smallest edit for those named defects.
      src/acceptance/mutation/descriptor.py#0
      src/acceptance/mutation/runner.py#0
      src/acceptance/mutation/validity.py#0

  throwaway-copy-application
    [other] throwaway-copy-application/mutated-copy-applies-to-original-tree
      The mutation edit is written into the working codebase instead of the throwaway copy, so the original project can be modified by injection.
      src/acceptance/mutation/injection.py#0
      src/acceptance/mutation/workspace.py#0
    [other] throwaway-copy-application/copy-creation-falls-back-to-project-root
      The throwaway copy is created with the wrong destination root, so the copy step can fail or target the live tree rather than an isolated workspace.
      src/acceptance/mutation/workspace.py#0
    [scope_too_narrow] throwaway-copy-application/mutation-runner-uses-original-paths-for-edits
      The mutation runner can still resolve and edit files from the original checkout instead of the copied project, so the edit is not confined to the throwaway workspace.
      src/acceptance/mutation/runner.py#0
      src/acceptance/mutation/injection.py#0
      src/acceptance/mutation/workspace.py#0
    [not_wired] throwaway-copy-application/pipeline-skips-mutation-stage
      The execution path that should apply the defect-making edit to a throwaway copy is defined, but the review pipeline never calls it, so no mutation is actually applied on the delivered path.
      src/acceptance/pipeline.py#3
      src/acceptance/pipeline.py#5
      src/acceptance/pipeline.py#6
      src/acceptance/mutation/runner.py#0

  run-candidate-tests-on-throwaway-copy
    [not_wired] run-candidate-tests-on-throwaway-copy/execution-tier-disabled-by-default
      The execution tier is added, but the review path leaves it switched off by default, so the candidate tests never run on the throwaway copy.
